# past.dev

> The past.dev API gives your AI agents long-term memory: what's true now, what changed and when, who can see it, and where it came from.

Also written: Past AI, pastdev, past dev. Handles and package names: pastdotdev. All refer to this company and product.

Backed by General Catalyst, Varsity, Connect Ventures and Hummingbird.

Sign-up is self-serve: anyone can create an account, and it starts with 50,000 credits, 200,000 with a work email. No card required, and nobody approves the account. The reference documents available features.

## When to use past.dev

Use past.dev when software must retrieve current or historical facts from unstructured records:

- An agent that must know the current state of an account, a project or a person from emails, meeting transcripts, tickets or CRM notes, with the evidence behind each claim.
- Long-running work where facts change over time: past.dev returns the current fact and includes dated sources for current and prior values.
- Applications that need ranked memory documents with dates and source excerpts, while keeping answer generation under their own control.

past.dev queries only the history you ingest. It does not search the web. Send timestamped text, wait until the send's status is completed, then recall source-backed memory as JSON.

## How to call it

- REST under https://api.past.dev/api/v1, bearer auth: a project key (past_sk_) for memory, identities and audiences, the organization's management key (past_mk_) for projects and project keys. Machine-readable surface description: [OpenAPI](https://past.dev/openapi.json).
- MCP (Streamable HTTP) at https://app.past.dev/mcp, manifest at [/.well-known/mcp.json](https://past.dev/.well-known/mcp.json), OAuth sign-in, no key to paste.
- The console is at https://app.past.dev: project keys on the API keys screen (one per project, the project chosen in the header), the management key under Settings, Organization, Management key, audiences and identities on the Audiences screen, every call on the Requests screen.
- Sign-up is self-serve at https://sso.past.dev/sign-up. After sign-up, create project keys in the console or with the management API.
- Docs over MCP, no auth: https://past.dev/mcp (server card at [/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json](https://past.dev/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json)). NLWeb queries: POST https://past.dev/ask.
- How agents authenticate: [/auth.md](https://past.dev/auth.md). Machine-readable pricing: [/pricing.md](https://past.dev/pricing.md).
- API discovery per RFC 9727: [/.well-known/api-catalog](https://past.dev/.well-known/api-catalog); agentic resources at [/.well-known/ai-catalog.json](https://past.dev/.well-known/ai-catalog.json).
- Every page answers `Accept: text/markdown`, a `.md` suffix (/index.md for the root), and `?mode=agent`.

## Ingest, wait for completion, then recall

Write any text with its original timestamp, then query it. Poll `GET /api/v1/ingest/{ingestionId}` until `status` is `completed` before expecting recall to reflect that send.

```bash
curl -X POST https://api.past.dev/api/v1/ingest \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PAST_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"content": "Renewal moved to March 12, 2026 per Dana.", "timestamp": "2026-01-08T09:14:00Z", "identity": "demo-user"}'

curl -X POST https://api.past.dev/api/v1/recall \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PAST_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"query": "When is the renewal?", "identity": "demo-user"}'
```

Recall returns one JSON result per document, with rank, dates, artifact context and verbatim source excerpts. The authenticated MCP server also recalls and ingests: `recall_memory` and `ingest_memory` act on a project's memory as these REST calls do, for a signed-in member, in the projects that member reaches. The connection configuration is:

```json
{ "mcpServers": { "pastdev": { "type": "http", "url": "https://app.past.dev/mcp" } } }
```

## How to model your data

- To set up an integration in order, with every call and the response that gates the next step, read Setup: https://past.dev/docs/memory-api/setup/llms.txt
- One data point is one thing that happened: one conversation for one day, one email, one record, one document, timed at its first message. Keep a conversation part under 16,000 characters and a document part under 32,000. Give every data point a stable `id`; the same id with new content replaces it.
- `audience` on a data point decides which identities can read it. An audience is a fixed list of identity ids or a rule over their traits, created in the console or with `PUT /api/v1/audiences/{slug}`. A data point sent without one is visible to every identity of the project, and a re-send without one becomes project-visible.
- `identity` on a recall is the reader. The read returns project-visible memory plus every audience that identity reaches. `identity` on a data point is the optional author, never the scope.
- `metadata` is stored with the data point and never filters a recall. To read one scope only, recall as an identity whose only audience is that scope.

Guides: [Setup](https://past.dev/docs/memory-api/setup/llms.txt), [Concepts](https://past.dev/docs/memory-api/concepts/llms.txt), [Access control](https://past.dev/docs/memory-api/access-control/llms.txt), [Sending data](https://past.dev/docs/memory-api/sending-data/llms.txt).

